      "logprobs": [],
      "text": "I’d be cautious. As of September 16, 2026, I cannot verify Business Intelligence DNA (businessinteldna.com) as an established, independently identifiable company.\n\nThe strongest finding is that the domain appears to be extremely new. A domain-registration index lists businessinteldna.com among .com domains newly registered around September 6, 2026—only about 10 days ago. ([com.all-url.info](https://com.all-url.info/21/25/?utm_source=openai)) The website is currently live and describes itself in Russian as “Business Intelligence DNA — интеллект-система для бизнеса и экспертов” (“an intelligence system for businesses and experts”). ([businessinteldna.com](https://businessinteldna.com/))\n\nMore concerning is what I could not substantiate. Searches for the exact domain and business name produced essentially no independent footprint: no clearly associated founders or executives, established LinkedIn company presence, press coverage, customer reviews, corporate history, or other third-party sources tying the website to a known legal entity. Searches for “Business Intelligence DNA” mostly return unrelated organizations and uses of those words. That doesn't establish that it's fraudulent, but it means there's currently very little independent evidence with which to establish legitimacy.\n\nI also couldn't identify who is actually behind the site from publicly indexed material. The public domain-registration route does not presently give me a verifiable individual/company owner; ICANN notes that registrant information can be nonpublic and that RDAP is the mechanism for current registration data. ([lookup.icann.org](https://lookup.icann.org/?utm_source=openai))\n\nSo I'd distinguish two questions:\n\n- Is it necessarily a scam? I don't have evidence sufficient to say that.\n- Is it a well-established, independently verifiable legitimate company? I don't see evidence of that either. The very recent domain plus lack of an identifiable operator or independent history are meaningful reasons for caution, particularly if they're asking you for money, financial information, identity documents, access to accounts, or an investment.\n\nIf someone from Business Intelligence DNA contacted you, I can investigate further. Send me the email/message you received (you can redact your personal details), the person's name, and what they're offering or asking you to do. I can check the individual, corporate-registration claims, contact information, payment methods, and other scam indicators."
